<clickhouse>
    <!--
        Read-only user for the query layer (one file per concern; mounted as a single file because the
        image entrypoint writes generated user files into /etc/clickhouse-server/users.d/).

        WHAT
        `posthog_ro` may only SELECT from the VIEW `posthog.project_events_v` and has NO privilege on
        `posthog.events` itself. The application's insight queries (ProjectScopedReader /
        ReadOnlyClickHouseClient) connect as this user, so a bug or a hostile query built there cannot
        write, alter, drop, read files or URLs, or look at other tables, and every read is limited to ONE
        project (see TENANT ISOLATION). The user name is fixed here (posthog_ro).

        TENANT ISOLATION (DEFINER view + row policy, FAIL CLOSED)
        The view is created by the migration database/clickhouse/migrations/002_scoped_events_view.sql with
        `DEFINER = CURRENT_USER SQL SECURITY DEFINER` (the admin) and the filter
            project_id = toUInt32(getSetting('SQL_project_id'))
        The setting SQL_project_id exists because config.d/10-custom-settings.xml declares the custom
        settings prefix SQL_. ProjectScopedReader sends it as a URL argument on every read. A query
        WITHOUT the setting does not return all rows: it fails (observed on 25.8: Code 115
        UNKNOWN_SETTING). A value that is not a UInt32 fails too (Code 6 or 72). The view reads
        posthog.events with the DEFINER's rights, which is why posthog_ro needs none. The primary key index
        still prunes through the view (getSetting folds to a constant: EXPLAIN indexes = 1 shows
        `project_id in [N, N]`).
        The `<databases><posthog><project_events_v><filter>` element below is the SAME condition as a row
        policy on the view, a second layer: it keeps the view fail closed even if a later migration
        recreates the view and forgets its WHERE (verified on 25.8: with the WHERE removed from the view the
        policy still rejects a read without the setting and still limits a read with it).
        The admin user `posthog` has no row policy and is unaffected (ingestion, materialized views,
        capture:status, migrations).

        WHAT IS CLOSED ON THE SERVER AND WHAT ONLY BY THE READER LINT (honest list)
        Closed on the server (verified on 25.8, also after a server restart): direct reads of
        posthog.events, mergeTreeIndex('posthog','events') (and mergeTreeProjection), merge() and view()
        over posthog.events all fail with ACCESS_DENIED (there is no grant); mergeTreeIndex on the view
        fails (it is not a MergeTree); a read without SQL_project_id fails; the old analyzer
        (enable_analyzer = 0) changes none of this; the table functions remote, cluster, url, file, s3 ...
        fail without the SOURCES/REMOTE grant, and input, executable, dictionary, loop fail without CREATE
        TEMPORARY TABLE; every other table has no grant. Needs no lint to be safe.
        NOT closed on the server (only the ProjectScopedReader lint and a typed-parameter discipline):
        `readonly=2` lets this user change settings, and a `const` constraint on SQL_project_id is
        impossible because it would also block the reader's own URL argument. So SQL that contains
        `SETTINGS SQL_project_id = <other id>` overrides the filter for that query (the integration test in
        tests/Feature/ClickHouseTenantIsolationTest.php pins this). The filter therefore protects against
        BUGS in query builders (forgotten filter, OR, UNION arm, a comment hiding a placeholder), NOT
        against SQL injection. Injection is prevented by typed {name:Type} parameters (no string literals
        in builder SQL), builder identifiers from allow lists, and the ProjectScopedReader lint (no
        SETTINGS, semicolon, comments, quotes, deny listed table functions or direct table names; caller
        settings starting with SQL_ are refused). Never let user text become SQL text.
        A way to close even that was seen in a scratch container but is NOT applied: a view filtering on
        getClientHTTPHeader('X-Project-Id') with `allow_get_client_http_header` pinned in this profile
        (SQL cannot change an HTTP header; it also prunes and fails closed without the header). It needs
        header support in the client, so it is a possible follow-up, not part of this design.

        DRIFT
        The view lists the columns of `events` explicitly (SELECT * would skip the MATERIALIZED ones). ALTER
        of events: ADD COLUMN leaves the view silently WITHOUT the new column, DROP/RENAME of a viewed column
        makes the view fail when read, a changed type stays stale in the view metadata. A migration that
        alters `events` must recreate the view (`CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW`, keeping DEFINER and the WHERE) and
        update ProjectScopedReader::COLUMNS; the opt-in test 'keeps ProjectScopedReader::COLUMNS equal to
        the real columns of posthog.events AND of the scoped view' fails loudly otherwise.

        WHY ONLY THE VIEW (least privilege)
        The scoped reader's contract covers events only, through the view. `events_errors` holds the RAW
        payloads of any project and has no project_id column; `kafka_events` is direct-select blocked
        anyway; `schema_migrations` is needless. A table created later must NOT become readable
        automatically, so the grant names the view instead of using posthog.*. Each new readable relation
        is one more <query> line below (for example Milestone 4 will add one for `person_distinct_ids`) and
        needs
            docker compose up -d - -force-recreate clickhouse
        (a new readable relation also needs its own DEFINER view or its own row policy `filter` under
        <databases>, otherwise it would be readable for every project).
        `capture:status` reads `events_errors` and `system.*` with the ADMIN client, so it is unaffected.

        NEVER add SOURCES, CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE or INTROSPECTION grants. `readonly=2` still allows
        CREATE TEMPORARY TABLE, and it is the ABSENCE of the SOURCES grant (not `readonly`) that blocks
        the url(), file() and remote() table functions. INTROSPECTION would open the introspection
        functions (allow_introspection_functions is pinned to 0 below as a second lock).

        PROFILE NAME
        The profile is called `ro_insights` and NOT `readonly`: the stock users.xml already defines a
        profile named `readonly` (readonly=1), and a same-named profile here would be merged with it
        instead of replacing it.

        WHY readonly=2 (and not 1)
        readonly=1 forbids changing ANY setting, which includes the URL arguments the HTTP client sends
        (default_format, wait_end_of_query, database). readonly=2 allows changing settings but never
        `readonly` itself, and the constraints below bound every limit that matters: a query may LOWER
        a limit or pass a benign setting, but cannot raise a limit above the profile maximum.

        LIMITS (sized for a roughly 2 GB box that also runs Kafka, Postgres and the app)
        Memory: max_memory_usage 400 MB per query, max_memory_usage_for_user 1 GB for all of the user's
        queries together, max_concurrent_queries_for_user 3. Arithmetic: Octane has 4 worker threads and
        a dashboard fires several queries in parallel; 3 concurrent queries at 400 MB is up to 1.2 GB
        in the worst case, so the 1 GB user wide cap is what actually bounds the sum. Memory pressure matters
        beyond the query itself: when the server runs short, the Kafka materialized view INSERT can fail
        with MEMORY_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, and a persistent MV insert failure wedges the Kafka consumer (the same
        wedge class described in 10-ingest-settings.xml). max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio does NOT
        protect the neighbouring containers (Kafka, Postgres, the app): the kernel OOM killer fires
        before it matters. A compose mem_limit for ClickHouse is a Milestone 8 item.
        Both user level settings (max_memory_usage_for_user, max_concurrent_queries_for_user) are
        0 = unlimited by default and settable by the caller, hence the min 1 constraint on them.

        Time: max_execution_time default 20 s, constraint cap 22 s. The chain is: server default 20 s,
        cap 22 s, HTTP client timeout 25 s (config clickhouse.readonly.timeout), Octane request limits
        above that, so the server cancels cleanly before the client gives up.

        Result size: max_result_rows 20000 and max_result_bytes 16 MB (builders may lower them per
        query). The reason is PHP, not ClickHouse: json_decode of a 256 MB result would exhaust PHP's
        memory_limit (256M, docker/zz-app.ini) BEFORE ClickHouse throws, and 100000 rows is far too many
        for a UI anyway. With result_overflow_mode throw an oversized result is an error, not a silently
        truncated answer.

        max_threads 4 keeps one query from taking every core. max_rows_to_read and max_bytes_to_read are
        backstops against full scans of a huge table; they throw instead of returning a silently
        truncated answer.

        Spilling to disk (external GROUP BY / sort, and the partial_merge / grace_hash join algorithms,
        which can write temp files) is switched off and pinned, so a heavy query hits max_memory_usage and
        fails instead of filling the (nearly full) Docker disk. join_algorithm is pinned to `hash`.
        max_temp_data_on_disk_size_for_query is a server level setting on 25.8, not a profile setting.

        Also pinned: log_queries 1 (the read-only user must not switch off its own audit trail in
        system.query_log), use_query_cache 0 (no cross-query result sharing), allow_introspection_functions
        0, stream_like_engine_allow_direct_select 0 (a direct SELECT from the Kafka engine table would
        consume messages away from the ingest consumer).

        Result shape pins (const, so neither the client nor SQL can change them; names validated on 25.8):
        session_timezone UTC: the server clock functions (now(), toDate(now())) and DateTime parsing without
        an explicit zone use UTC for this user, whatever the server zone is. The PROJECT zone is always passed
        explicitly by the query builders (toStartOfInterval(..., {tz:String})), never through this setting.
        output_format_json_quote_64bit_integers 0: UInt64/Int64 (count(), uniqExact()) come back as JSON
        NUMBERS (25.8 default is also 0; pinned so a server upgrade cannot turn counts into strings). PHP
        decodes them as int up to 2^63-1 and as float above, which counts never reach; do not select raw
        UInt64 hashes (cityHash64 etc.) through this user.
        serialize_query_plan 0: the query plan is not serialized for distributed execution (nothing here is
        distributed; pinned to keep the plan, and EXPLAIN output, in the form the tests expect).
        enable_analyzer 1: the new analyzer is the default on 25.8; pinning it stops a query from switching
        to the old analyzer, whose view, alias and subquery semantics differ (the tenant isolation tests
        were verified on the new one).

        WHY EVERY LIMIT CONSTRAINT HAS min 1
        For these settings 0 means UNLIMITED, and 0 is numerically below any max, so a bare max
        constraint would let a query pass `max_execution_time = 0` or `max_result_rows = 0` and switch the
        limit off (verified on 25.8). min 1 closes that hole; the client may still lower a limit.

        PASSWORD
        `from_env` reads the password from the CLICKHOUSE_RO_PASSWORD environment variable of the
        clickhouse container (compose passes it, default posthog_ro). If the variable is NOT SET in the
        container the server fails to load the config and does not start; an empty value would mean an
        EMPTY PASSWORD, so keep the explicit non-empty default in docker-compose.yml. Change it before the
        first public start: set CLICKHOUSE_RO_PASSWORD in .env, then run
            docker compose up -d - -force-recreate clickhouse
        and `docker compose up -d` (the app container reads the same variable). The account is defined in
        this file, not in the data volume, so no migration is needed.

        STARTUP TRAP
        An unknown or renamed setting in the profile (or constraint) ABORTS server start: the container
        exits with code 91 and restarts in a loop. Read `docker compose logs clickhouse` for the offending
        name. Validate any edit in a throwaway container first (docker run with this file mounted) before
        touching the live service.

        NOTE ON COMMANDS
        XML comments cannot contain two consecutive hyphens, so long options below are written with a
        space after the first hyphen: read '- -force-recreate' as two hyphens followed by force-recreate.

        ROLLOUT ORDER (existing volume)
        1. docker compose up -d - -force-recreate clickhouse   (new grant: the view)
        2. the app container runs `clickhouse:migrate` at every start (docker/start-app.sh), which applies
           002_scoped_events_view.sql; or run `docker compose exec -T app php artisan clickhouse:migrate`.
        Until step 2 the read-only user cannot read events (the view does not exist yet); capture is
        unaffected. A fresh volume gets both automatically.

        HOW TO APPLY
        This is a SINGLE-FILE bind mount, which follows the inode. Editing the file in place (sed -i,
        most editors) or `git pull` / checkout (unlink + create) is NOT picked up by
        `docker compose up -d`. Run:
            docker compose up -d - -force-recreate clickhouse
    -->
    <profiles>
        <ro_insights>
            <readonly>2</readonly>
            <max_execution_time>20</max_execution_time>
            <timeout_overflow_mode>throw</timeout_overflow_mode>
            <max_memory_usage>400000000</max_memory_usage>
            <max_memory_usage_for_user>1000000000</max_memory_usage_for_user>
            <max_concurrent_queries_for_user>3</max_concurrent_queries_for_user>
            <max_threads>4</max_threads>
            <max_result_rows>20000</max_result_rows>
            <max_result_bytes>16777216</max_result_bytes>
            <result_overflow_mode>throw</result_overflow_mode>
            <max_rows_to_read>1000000000</max_rows_to_read>
            <max_bytes_to_read>64000000000</max_bytes_to_read>
            <read_overflow_mode>throw</read_overflow_mode>
            <max_bytes_before_external_group_by>0</max_bytes_before_external_group_by>
            <max_bytes_ratio_before_external_group_by>0</max_bytes_ratio_before_external_group_by>
            <max_bytes_before_external_sort>0</max_bytes_before_external_sort>
            <max_bytes_ratio_before_external_sort>0</max_bytes_ratio_before_external_sort>
            <join_algorithm>hash</join_algorithm>
            <log_queries>1</log_queries>
            <use_query_cache>0</use_query_cache>
            <allow_introspection_functions>0</allow_introspection_functions>
            <stream_like_engine_allow_direct_select>0</stream_like_engine_allow_direct_select>
            <session_timezone>UTC</session_timezone>
            <output_format_json_quote_64bit_integers>0</output_format_json_quote_64bit_integers>
            <serialize_query_plan>0</serialize_query_plan>
            <enable_analyzer>1</enable_analyzer>

            <constraints>
                <readonly><const/></readonly>
                <max_execution_time><min>1</min><max>22</max></max_execution_time>
                <timeout_overflow_mode><const/></timeout_overflow_mode>
                <max_memory_usage><min>1</min><max>400000000</max></max_memory_usage>
                <max_memory_usage_for_user><min>1</min><max>1000000000</max></max_memory_usage_for_user>
                <max_concurrent_queries_for_user><min>1</min><max>3</max></max_concurrent_queries_for_user>
                <max_threads><min>1</min><max>4</max></max_threads>
                <max_result_rows><min>1</min><max>20000</max></max_result_rows>
                <max_result_bytes><min>1</min><max>16777216</max></max_result_bytes>
                <result_overflow_mode><const/></result_overflow_mode>
                <max_rows_to_read><min>1</min><max>1000000000</max></max_rows_to_read>
                <max_bytes_to_read><min>1</min><max>64000000000</max></max_bytes_to_read>
                <read_overflow_mode><const/></read_overflow_mode>
                <max_bytes_before_external_group_by><const/></max_bytes_before_external_group_by>
                <max_bytes_ratio_before_external_group_by><const/></max_bytes_ratio_before_external_group_by>
                <max_bytes_before_external_sort><const/></max_bytes_before_external_sort>
                <max_bytes_ratio_before_external_sort><const/></max_bytes_ratio_before_external_sort>
                <join_algorithm><const/></join_algorithm>
                <log_queries><const/></log_queries>
                <use_query_cache><const/></use_query_cache>
                <allow_introspection_functions><const/></allow_introspection_functions>
                <stream_like_engine_allow_direct_select><const/></stream_like_engine_allow_direct_select>
                <session_timezone><const/></session_timezone>
                <output_format_json_quote_64bit_integers><const/></output_format_json_quote_64bit_integers>
                <serialize_query_plan><const/></serialize_query_plan>
                <enable_analyzer><const/></enable_analyzer>
            </constraints>
        </ro_insights>
    </profiles>

    <users>
        <posthog_ro>
            <password from_env="CLICKHOUSE_RO_PASSWORD"/>
            <profile>ro_insights</profile>
            <quota>default</quota>
            <networks>
                <ip>::/0</ip>
            </networks>
            <grants>
                <query>GRANT SELECT ON posthog.project_events_v</query>
            </grants>
            <databases>
                <posthog>
                    <project_events_v>
                        <filter>project_id = toUInt32(getSetting('SQL_project_id'))</filter>
                    </project_events_v>
                </posthog>
            </databases>
        </posthog_ro>
    </users>
</clickhouse>
